# Two symbols. MB_PEXT is PEXT_SPEC.md section 7.2's build-time half of the
# heterogeneity contract, copied verbatim from samples/modelblaster_pext so the two
# images select kernels the same way. TACIT_MB_SINK_DMA is what lets one source build
# for the board and for spike.

mainmenu "ModelBlaster LeNet under TACIT, with the MBP packed-integer extension"

config MB_PEXT
	bool "Build the ModelBlaster kernels against the MBP extension"
	default y
	help
	  Emit the real MBP.DOT8 / MBP.MAX8 / MBP.QMUL / MBP.CLIP8 encodings
	  (custom-0, opcode 0x0b) instead of pext.h's software model.

	  THE EXTENSION EXISTS ON HART 0 ONLY.  With this enabled, any thread
	  that reaches a curated pext kernel MUST be pinned to CPU 0 with
	  k_thread_cpu_pin(), or it takes an illegal-instruction trap.  See
	  PEXT_SPEC.md section 7.

	  Turn this off and the identical sources compile against the header's
	  bit-identical software model -- useful for bring-up, and it makes the
	  traced window contain no custom-0 encodings at all, which is the one
	  thing this sample exists not to report.

config TACIT_MB_SINK_DMA
	bool "Stream the encoded trace into DRAM through TraceSinkDMA"
	default y
	help
	  y  -- the FPGA SoC.  PynqZ2RocketBigLittlePextTacitConfig instantiates
	        tacit.WithTraceSinkDMA(1) and nothing else, so the encoder is
	        pointed at target 1, the sink is given a DRAM address, and the
	        guest flushes the inclusive L2 afterwards because the sink is a
	        master on the system bus and the PS reads DRAM.  See
	        fpga/pynq-z2/docs/TACIT_ON_FPGA.md sections 2 and 3.

	  n  -- spike.  The simulator's sink is target 0 and writes tacit.out,
	        tacit.log and tacit.debug into the CWD; it needs no address, and
	        there is no L2 control node at 0x2010000 to write to (the store
	        would take an access fault).  Set from
	        boards/spike_riscv64.conf.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
